        {
            "id": "memory-safety",
            "title": "Rust-Style Ownership & Borrow Checker",
            "badge": "Memory Safety",
            "description": "Single ownership, moves for heap types, borrowing with udhaar and udhaar_badal, explicit nakal, and RAII alvida destructors.",
            "content": [
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "MEMElang brings Rust-level memory safety to Desi programming. Say goodbye to use-after-free, double drops, and data races! Values have single owners; assignments of heap types move ownership by default; references are checked at runtime with hilarious Bollywood errors."
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": 2,
                    "text": "1. Single Ownership & Move Semantics"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Primitive types (`int`, `float`, `bool`, `nalla`) have `Copy` semantics. Heap and compound resources (`string`, `biradari` object instances, and closures) have Move semantics. Assigning or passing them transfers ownership permanently:"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "title": "move_semantics.meme",
                    "code": "pakad a = \"Baburao ka khazana\";\npakad b = a; // Ownership transferred to b!\n\n// Reading 'a' now throws a borrow-checker error:\n// dikha a; // Error: Pehli fursat mein nikal! 'a' ki value move ho chuki hai!\n\ndikha \"Naya owner b:\", b;\n\n// Re-assigning re-initializes 'a':\na = \"Naye sapne\";\ndikha \"Reinitialized a:\", a;"
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": 2,
                    "text": "2. Explicit Cloning with nakal()"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "To duplicate a heap value without giving up ownership of the original, use `nakal(val)` (or method `val.nakal()`, aliases: `clone`, `copy`):"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "title": "explicit_clone.meme",
                    "code": "pakad original = \"Diamond Kohinoor\";\npakad duplicate = nakal(original); // Cloned!\n\ndikha \"Original:\", original; // Still valid!\ndikha \"Duplicate:\", duplicate;\n\nbiradari Khazana {\n    jugaad shuru(coins) {\n        apun.coins = coins;\n    }\n}\n\npakad k1 = Khazana(100);\npakad k2 = k1.nakal(); // Deep clone of instance!\nk2.coins = 500;\n\ndikha \"k1 coins:\", k1.coins; // 100\ndikha \"k2 coins:\", k2.coins; // 500"
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": 2,
                    "text": "3. Shared Borrowing (udhaar) vs Mutable Borrowing (udhaar_badal)"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Instead of moving ownership, you can lend references using Aliasing XOR Mutability rules:\n- `udhaar x` (`&x`): Immutable shared borrow. Multiple shared borrows can coexist. `x` cannot be mutated or moved while borrowed!\n- `udhaar_badal x` (`&mut x`): Exclusive mutable borrow. Only one can exist at a time; direct access to `x` is suspended until the borrow ends!"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "title": "borrowing.meme",
                    "code": "pakad hero = \"Munna Don\";\n\n// 1. Shared Borrowing:\npakad r1 = udhaar hero;\npakad r2 = udhaar hero;\ndikha \"Read via ref1:\", r1;\ndikha \"Read via ref2:\", r2;\n\n// hero = \"Circuit\"; // Error: Doglapan! 'hero' abhi udhaar pe hai!\n\n// 2. Mutable Borrowing in Scopes:\npakad score = 100;\n{\n    pakad m = udhaar_badal score;\n    m = 250; // Mutate through reference!\n    dikha \"Inside scope modified score:\", m;\n}\n// Block exited, borrow released!\ndikha \"Outside scope score:\", score; // 250"
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": 2,
                    "text": "4. RAII Scope Destructors (alvida)"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Classes can define a destructor method `jugaad alvida() { ... }` (alias: `drop`). MEMElang automatically invokes this destructor when an instance's owner block scope ends!"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "title": "raii_destructors.meme",
                    "code": "biradari FileLocker {\n    jugaad shuru(filename) {\n        apun.filename = filename;\n        dikha \"[Open] File opened:\", apun.filename;\n    }\n    jugaad alvida() {\n        dikha \"[Lock] File closed cleanly (RAII):\", apun.filename;\n    }\n}\n\n{\n    pakad locker = FileLocker(\"confidential.docx\");\n    dikha \"Doing important file processing...\";\n}\n// When scope exits, alvida() is called automatically!\ndikha \"Scope ended cleanly!\";"
                }
            ]
        },
